  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nThe latest satellite imagery reveals a large upper-level low \nslowly rotating east across Quebec this afternoon, with \nlingering weak surface troughing found over the Eastern Great \nLakes. Radar imagery reveals mainly isolated to scattered light \nlake effect rain showers across portions of Northeast Ohio and \nNorthwest Pennsylvania this afternoon. Coverage is expected to \nremain mainly isolated to scattered as low-level moisture \nremains limited. Conditions will gradually improve throughout \nthe day Tuesday as high pressure builds in from the west, \nresulting in a wide array of temperatures ranging from the low \n80s across Northwest Ohio, to the upper 60s or low 70s across \nNorthwest Pennsylvania. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2.. \nThe next system will cross the area Wednesday night into\nThursday, extending a cold front across the region Thursday\nnight. Rain chances will first increase Wednesday night along\nand west of the I-75 corridor as decaying showers and \nthunderstorms begin to move east into the area, associated with\na pre-frontal shortwave. Still some uncertainty on the timing of\nthis feature, though the latest guidance favors redevelopment \nof this activity Thursday afternoon, generally east of the I-71 \ncorridor. There is some support for some stronger storms to\ndevelop ahead of the front Thursday afternoon and evening in\nthis area, aided by a 25 to 30-knot SW 700 mb jet, though the\noverall threat for a more organized severe weather threat\nremains lows at this time.",
